  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "\\(2^{-(2k+1)}-2^{-(2k-1)}+2^{-2k}\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2^{-2k}\\)",
    "B": "\\(2^{-(2k-1)}\\)",
    "C": "\\(-2^{-(2k+1)}\\)",
    "D": "\\(0\\)",
    "E": "\\(2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The exponents differ by only \\(1\\) or \\(2\\), so express every term using the same power. Let \\(t=2^{-(2k+1)}\\). The exponent-sum rule gives\n\\[\n2^{-(2k-1)}=2^{-(2k+1)+2}=4t\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n2^{-2k}=2^{-(2k+1)+1}=2t.\n\\]\nTherefore the original expression is\n\\[\nt-4t+2t=-t=\\boxed{-2^{-(2k+1)}}.\n\\]\nThese identities hold for every real \\(k\\), since the base \\(2\\) is positive. The answer is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
